{
 "description": "The map of the UBL example world: routes, towns and villages, the offshore platform and the pipelines' metering stations, on a grid of 256 x 192 squares of 3.5 km (tools/world_model.py, GRID). tools/world_model.py builds the tile model from it, from the parties and from the terrain (world/terrain.json: land, sea, river, estuary, land cover, countries and heights, made by tools/terrain.py from world/terrain-inputs.json, O152). Stations are the pipelines' border metering stations. Towns and localities (villages) give their population (for the villages the decided range) and the width of their built-up area in km (built_km, owner 2026-11-18, O92): the model draws a built-up area of that size around the centre c. Localities give the parties outside the towns a place in their address. A town may have a harbour of its own (a quay square and the side its water lies on), without a party: Larkmouth's. Roads, rails and pipelines are given square by square, each square a neighbour of the last, diagonals included, as tools/lay_routes.py laid them over the terrain (O152 stage 3: least-cost ways with the gradient of their class, a main road about 6 to 7 %, a minor road up to 10 %, a railway 1.5 to 2 %, following the valleys and crossing ridges at their passes; tools/routing.py): a road's \"class\" is main or minor, \"bridges\" and \"tunnels\" list the squares a route spans or runs under; the access roads to the parties are routes too (\"access-<party>\"). Do not edit these by hand: change the network in tools/lay_routes.py and run it. The river route names the barges' first quay, and the model follows the river's squares from there to the estuary, below the rapids above Ferrano. The drawn map is a diagram (O82): it may stretch distances, and Lisavu, about 1,000 km east of Lumeport, is drawn nearer, in its own part of the grid with open sea all round, as an inset.",
